---
// site-v2 work order §3 (research-ecology docs/design/site-v2-decisions-2026-07-16.md §1.3):
// the hub is now the entrance — hero (the pulse) → the current encounter, condensed → the four
// doors → where works travel → also on this site. The previous entrance (the full score/
// Partitur experience) moved to its own stable URL, /begegnungen. Approval trail for both this
// change and the score-entrance it replaces: docs/decision-log.md. Git is the archive — the
// prior versions of this file live in its history, not as commented-out blocks; the old
// Home.astro/HeroField.astro components stay in the repo, unlinked (git log for provenance).
// 2026-08-11: the entrance became an OPS ROOM (design handoff option 3a,
// docs/design_handoff_homepage_ops_room/README.md) — a status board of everything running here
// instead of a page of prose about it. HubEntrance.astro stays in the repo, unlinked, the same way
// Home.astro/HeroField.astro did before it: Git is the archive, and the approval trail for this
// change is in docs/decision-log.md.
// 2026-09-01, morning: the entrance became the FRONT DOOR — the ops room's full board (rows,
// sparklines, tiles) moved whole to /now (NowBoard.astro), and the entrance kept a clear personal
// opening, a slim live strip and an editorial front page of the newest works.
// 2026-09-01, evening: the OPS ROOM RETURNS to the entrance — Frank's decision (wording private):
// the board with every lab tile is the page he wants to open on, and it should be handsomer than
// before, with more of the drawing done by d3. FrontDoor.astro stays in the repo, unlinked and
// dated, like HubEntrance/Home before it: Git is the archive. /now keeps the board page as built
// that morning. The beauty pass is in the room itself (OpsRoom.astro, src/lib/ops/*).
// 2026-10-11: the entrance became an EDITION (design handoff docs/design_handoff_homepage_ecology/
// README.md) — the same records, laid out like the front page of a paper on a paper ground: the
// standing question, the pulse, what is new since yesterday or since the reader's last visit,
// the ecology's running cycle beside a timetable of who worked when, the other houses, the
// readings, the arrivals, an index. OpsRoom.astro stays in the repo, unlinked and dated, the
// way its predecessors did; /now keeps its board. Approval trail: docs/decision-log.md.
// 2026-11-02: the edition leads with the lab — a carousel of the lab's experiments under the
// masthead, and the selected works under it holding the practices' pieces only (Frank's decision,
// wording private; docs/decision-log.md). Everything else on the page follows them unchanged.
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'
import HomeEdition from '@/components/pages/HomeEdition.astro'
import { NAMING } from '@/config/naming'
import { SITE } from '@/lib/site'

// SEO: the home page is the authority page for the name query Frank Bültge. Title and
// description therefore lead with name and role (the strongest entity signal for a name query),
// and the concept follows as context. Frank had dropped in the ranking for his own name after
// site-v2 (2026-07-16) moved title, H1 and description to the abstract concept; this restores
// the person signal without touching the design. The role is SITE.role, the same on every
// surface (its history: docs/wording-kanon.md).
// 2026-08-12: the ops-room rebuild of 2026-08-11 had dropped the name from the entrance (it
// rendered `focusKicker` instead of `eyebrow`), so it stood only in nav and footer. The eyebrow
// came back, and naming.test.ts holds it: on the name query this site competes with a namesake
// established since 2007 and cannot afford to leave its own name out.
// 2026-10-11: The name line moved from the entrance's own eyebrow to the left end of the top
// bar (TopBar.astro renders NAMING.eyebrow), where it stands on every page, this one included;
// naming.test.ts and front-door.test.ts hold it there.
const seoTitle = `Frank Bültge — ${SITE.role.en} · ${NAMING.title}`
const seoDescription =
  `Frank Bültge — ${SITE.role.en}. Projects and experiments on how AI and automation can support and extend artistic research — machine-run practices, instruments and studies, every claim tied to its evidence. Among them: a federated research ecology, Machine Attention, datavism.org and data-snack.com. Git is the archive.`
---
<Page title={seoTitle} description={seoDescription} surface="paper">
  <HomeEdition />
</Page>
